<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1124>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "1124";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "The desire to be leaner to improve sports performance was predictive of DE and not vice versa. Emotional distress resulting from missed exercise sessions and social pressure from the sports environment had no significant impact on DE. Sports-related body disssatisfaction is not predictive of DE when other sports-related variables are also considered. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1124>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1124>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics%2C_ice_figure_skating%2C_diving%2C_ballet%2C_roller-skate_figure_skating%2C_rhythmic_gymnastics>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "The rather short follow-up period of 1 year. Owing to our rather small sample size, the generalization of our results is further limited. Our study focuses on only one high-risk group. High performance athletes might not report pathological eating patterns because they fear being excluded from their team sports. Inclusion of only four potential risk factors";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "It would be interesting to investigate whther the desire to be leaner to improve sports performance is a risk factor for disordered eating in other types and how this desire is related to other (also non-sports-related) risk factors. More studies with a longitudinal design are needed to deepen our knowledge of the developmental course of disordered eating in athletes. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "Emotional distress resulting from missed exercise sessions 𝛃=.22, p=.10\nSocial pressure from the sport environment 𝛃=.23, p=.06\nDesire to be leaner to improve sports performance 𝛃=.27, p=.04\nSports related body dissatisfaction 𝛃=-.06, p=.62\nR²=.31";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "65";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Multiple linear regression" .

