        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Cross-sectional design, precluding examination of the directionality of the associations between SPA and DEAB. This study focused on the use of DEAB as a coping strategy for physical self-presentational concerns related to thinness or leanness. The fact that this study fails to consider concerns specifically related to muscularity and their possible relation with DEAB is an important limitation. No distinction was made for sports where the main emphasis is on muscularity, weight gain, or even drastric weight control (e.g., body building, football, power lifting). Less than half of the adolescents were involved in competitive sports and most did so at a nonelite level. We cannot identify the moderating role of the type of competition on the relationship between SPA and DEAB. This study neglected to examine more pathogenic behaviors (i.e., fasting, use of laxatives and/or diuretics, enemas, appetite suppressants) that adolescents could use to their physique. We do not know whether SPA is associated with the pathogenic weight control behaviors that adolescents engage in or whether sport practice is a moderator of this relationship. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Consideration of the potential impact on muscularity oriented and weight control sports in the relationships between SPA, gender and DEAB should be the object of future studies. \nCulture is known to play an important role in the definition of body shape ideals and thus may exert a significant impact on the relationships observed in the current study. \nTo further extend the current results, it would be interesting for future research to focus more specifically on the role of (a) body related sport motives (i.e., weight and shape control, weight loss, improvement of ones appearance), (b) social comparison processes and/or social physique pressure, and (c) socio contextual factors (parents, peers, coaches) of sport practices in the relationships between SPA, gender, sport practice and DEAB. \nOne strategy that clinicians could adopt to prevent or reduce the development of high levels of DEAB in adolescents would be to focus on the reduction of high levels of physical self-presentation concerns ; adopt healthy coping strategies for dealing with SPA levels. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "Age 𝛃=-.33, p<.001\nBody mass index 𝛃=.09, N.S.\nSocial physique anxiety 𝛃=.15, p<.001\nGender 𝛃=-.88, p<.05 \nInvolvement in sport practise 𝛃=.42, N.S.\nR²=.08";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "766";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Mixed sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Hierarchical multivariate regression" .

