<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1474>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "1474";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Rhytmic gymnasts were considered to be at a greater risk for ED development than were artistic gymnasts or sport acrobats. This findings is probably due to sport-specific pressure in rhythmic gymnastics to obtain a very thin body rather than other variables such as age or perfectionist tendencies. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1474>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1474>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics_%28artistic%2C_rhythmic%2C_sport_acrobatics%29>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Sample small. Only 6 on the 9 coaches who were approached agreed to participate in the study. It is possible that only those coaches who were certain that their group was altogether healthy would be more likely to agree to take part, and vice versa. Secrecy, deception and denial are considered inherent to eating disorders, and it is generally agreed that self report measures for eating disorders tend to yield conservative estimates. Underreporting also constitues a possible limitation. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "The climate in training can be an important variable in eating disorder development. An investigation into motivational climates and perceived sport specific pressures would be a welcome addition to the eating disturbances literature. \nQualitative research methologies may be used to gain a deeper insight into what pressures are present, as well as their sources, such as coaches, parents, teammates, judges, and others. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.20, N.S.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NS";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "50";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Correlation" .

