        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Basketball%2C_cross-country%2C_diving%2C_gymnastics%2C_lacrosse%2C_soccer%2C_swimming_and_track_and_field>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "A comprehensive understanding of sociocultural influences on disordered eating among athletes require not only greater representation of participants from marginazlied racial and ethnic groups, but also consideration of the role of intersecting marginalized identities (e.g., sexual orientation) and integration of qualitative and mixed methodological approaches that may more effectively characterizz the lived experiences of these athletes. Cross sectional design ; it is not possible to determine whether these characteristics are risk factors for disordered eating or correlates of these behaviors. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                " \nInterventions that specifically address the sociocultural pressures and appearance ideals most relevant to athletes, such as the Female Athlete Body Project, reduce risk for subsequent disordered eating behaviors. ED prevention programs for Female athletes should address general sociocultural pressures related to weight and appearance. These interventions should also address internalization of cultural ideals for appearance beyond thinness; for example dissonance-based interventions could challenge internalization of fit and muscular ideals and sport specific ideals for weight and shape. Interventions could also be enhanced by strengths-based approaches that build on elements of sport participation that appear to be protective, such as the contribution of athletes positive evaluations of their physical abilities and condition to body esteem. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "Sport specific weight pressures-general weight pressures 𝛃=.48, p<.001\nGeneral weight pressures-thin ideal internalization 𝛃=.55, p<.001\nSport specific weight pressures-Thin ideal internalization 𝛃=.06, p=.450\nGeneral weight pressures-body dissatisfaction 𝛃=.72, p<.001\nSport specific weight pressures-Body dissatisfaction 𝛃=.09, p=.240\nThin ideal internalization-Body dissatisfaction 𝛃=.04, p=.603\nMuscular ideal internalization-Body dissatisfaction 𝛃=-.07, p=.309\nGeneral weight pressures-muscular ideal internalization 𝛃=.48, p<.001\nSport specific weight pressures-Muscular ideal internalization 𝛃=.02, p=.832\nMuscular ideal internalization-thin ideal internalization 𝛃=.36, p<.001\nThin ideal internalization-disordered eating 𝛃=.22, p<.01\nMuscular ideal internalization-disordered eating 𝛃=.19, p<.01\nGeneral weight pressures-Disordered eating- 𝛃=.15, p=.293\nSport specific weight pressures-Disordered eating- 𝛃=.12, p=.165\nThin ideal internalization-Disordered eating- 𝛃=.22, p=.001\nMuscular ideal internalization-Disordered eating- 𝛃=.19, p<.001\nBody dissatisfaction-Disordered eating 𝛃=.17, p=.105\nR²=.42, p<.001\nχ2(33) = 69.11, p < .001; CFI = 0.95; TLI = 0.92; RMSEA = 0.07; SRMR = 0.05";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
