                "r=.71, p<.0001" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1800>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "1800";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Perfectionism and body satisfaction explain dieting among Female athletes. For the male athletes, none of the predictors contributed to the understanding of their dieting behaviour. In addition, age, sport type and duration of sport participation did not contribute to dieting behaviour un case of male and Female. Adaptive and maladaptive perfectionism were associated with body satisfaction among Female athletes but not with dieting behaviour directly. Satisfaction with one's body was shown to be the largest independent predictor of dieting and their relationship was negative. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1800>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1800>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Soccer%3B_basketball%3B_gymnastics%3B_figure_skating%3B_athletics%3B_swimming>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "The data was based on self reported measures. Cross sectional design limits the causal inference between perfectionism, body satisfaction and dieting. The sample consisted of athletes who were enrolled in a university program which might limit the generalizability. PANPS should not be used as a measure of perfectionism due to a questionable factor validity of the positive perfectionism subscale. EAT-26 was shown to have a low positive predictive value due to the rarity of targeted conditions while two out of three EAT-26 subscales demonstrated low internal consistency and omitting them while investigating eating disorder may not be the best approach. The number of Female participants was lower than males, which might have affected some of the analysis were gender-split was included. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "It would be important to further etablish the relationship between perfectionism dimensions and body satisfaction in the area of disordered eating but also take a consideration the context in which athletes perceive their body. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "\nAge 𝛃=.14, N.S.\nSport type 𝛃=.09, N.S.\nSport participation 𝛃=-.13, N.S.\nAdaptive perfectionism 𝛃=-.02, N.S.\nMaladaptive perfectionism 𝛃=.23, N.S.\nR²=.10";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "79";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Mixed sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Hierarchical multiple regression" .

