<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1839>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "1839";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Drive for muscularity was positively associated was associated with binge eating and restricting. While we did find an association between body satisfaction and restricting eating behavior, there was not a significant association among binge eating, purging, and body satisfaction. Coaches/teammates were only a significant predictor of restricting behavior and were the least important predictor of it compared to general pressures and sport appearance pressures. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1839>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1839>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Wrestling>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "The majority of participants identiied as white ; the present study sample is not representative of the racial makeup of this population. There was a relatively low response rate to the survey, which limits the generalizability of our sample. This study was cross sectional and only examined athletes in season, which limits the conclusions we can draw. The use of self report measures means it is possible that participants downplayed some of their behaviors or were unaware of the frequency of them. In addition, we did not ask specifically about frequency of binge eating. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "One possible intervention is by providing coaches and athletes a decision tree to plan a safe weight-cutting strategu such as consuming strategic level of carbohydrates and fluids. It is important that wrestlers evaluate their weight-cutting and recovery practices before and after competitions to assess what is working safely. \nFuture research should prioritize longitudinal studies that assess these variables in season versus out of season. Body satisfaction and disordered eating should be investigated in a racially diverse sample for both generalizability and differential risk. Future studies should examine Female's wrestling and explore gender differences in body satisfaction and disordered eaating behaviors. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "Raw relative weight\nGeneral pressures RawRelativeWeight=.14, p<.05 ; Rescaled_x000D_Relative_x000D_Weight=48.39, p<.05\nCoach/teammate pressures RawRelativeWeight=.06, p<.05; Rescaled_x000D_Relative_x000D_Weight=21.28, p<.05\nSport appearance pressures RawRelativeWeight=.09, p<.05;  Rescaled\nRelative Weight=30.33, p<.05\nAnalogue R² value=.29\n";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "103";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Relative weight analysis " .

