<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2130>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "2130";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Self-oriented perfectionism, through obsessive passion was related to higher levels of disordered eating. We did not find mediating effects for obsessive passion in the relationship between socially prescribed perfectionism and disordered eating, and for harmonious passion in the relationship between self oriented perfectionism and physical well-being or disordered eating. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2130>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2130>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics%2C_artistic_swimming>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Self reported measures. Recruitment of participants via MTurk may limit the generalizability of the representativeness. Although study 2 used a longitudinal design, there were only two timepoints and the sample size was relatively small. The instrument used was not intended to diagnose eating disorders. Important markers of eating psychopathology were not used. We included both recreational and competitive athletes. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Further research is deemed necessary to replicate the results of\nthis research and further extend our understanding of the psychological\nprocesses involved in disordered eating and physical and psychological\nwell-being in a broader set of sports and participants.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
