<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_215>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "215";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "This study did not find significant correlations between criticism received on social media and disordered eating behaviors in college gymnasts. Combining variables related to social hassles, body satisfaction, usage of certain social media outlets, and years of participation could perdict disordered eating. No relationship were found between social media variables and disordered eating behaviors. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_215>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_215>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "The main limitation of this study was the sample size of the participants. Although measures were exhausted in the recruitmalet of participants; it may have been beneficial to have sent the recruitmalet materials out earlier in the off season as opposed to a month before the college gymnastics season started. Another limitation was the limited reliability data for the Social Hassles Questionnaire, as it is relatively new instrument and has not been extensively tested. Furthermore this study did not account for mental health status beyond possible disordered eating behaviors. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Since depression and anxiety have been linked to disordered eating patterns, future studies should also consider these pathologies, along with self-esteem. Research should aim to better understand the relationship between social media criticism and disordered eating behaviors in athletic populations. Athletes from different sports may respond differently to criticism than gymnasts, so it could be valuable to study these variables in other aesthetic sports, such as figure skating, diving, cheerleading, or even non-aesthetic sports like softball or basketball. Additionally, exploring these factors in males involved in aesthetic sports and the overall impact of social media on athletes' mental health could provide important insights.\n\nIt would also be beneficial for athletes, parents, and other significant figures in athletes' lives to be trained in recognizing the signs of disordered eating behaviors. Such training could help raise awareness of the potential dangers of social media platforms like Snapchat and offer guidance on how to support athletes if disordered eating behaviors arise.\n\nTo counter the effects of social media criticism on athletes, education initiatives should target coaches, families, friends, and athletes themselves to prevent the development of harmful eating behaviors and patterns, both now and in the future.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.722, p<.01";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "+";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "72";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_215>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Population>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/gender>
                "Female";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasStatistics>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/AgeStats_215> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/BmiStats_215> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/ExFreqStats_215> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/ExpStats_215>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSubgroup>
                "N.A.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/inclusionCriteria>
                "Participants were recruited from club gyms and colleges throughout the united states via email, social media, and word of mouth to coaches, support staff and directly to athletes; Females between 18-25 years; self identify as competitive gymnasts; must have competed within the last three years";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/population>
                "Athletes";
