                "χ² (24, N =334) =69.28, p <.001; χ²/df =2.89; CFI =0.96; TLI =.88; RMSEA =0.075 (90 % CI [0.055, 0.096]); SRMR =0.060 Pressure from family-thin ideal internalization 𝛃=.13, p<.05 Pressure from family-athletic ideal internalization 𝛃=-.13, p<.05 Peer pressure-athletic ideal internalization 𝛃=.14, p<.05 Pressure from media-thin ideal internalization 𝛃=.36, p<.001 Pressure from media-appearance comparison 𝛃=.56, p<.001 Pressure from teammate-athletic ideal internalization 𝛃=.26, p<.05 Thin ideal internalization-restrictive eating 𝛃=.18, p<.01 Thin ideal internalization-cognitive restraint 𝛃=.24, p<.001 Thin ideal internalization-body dissatisfaction 𝛃=.90, p<.001 Thin ideal internalization-binge eating 𝛃=.12, p<.05 Appearance comparison-restrictive eating 𝛃=.23, p<.001 Appearance comparison-cognitive restraint 𝛃=.19, p<.01 Appearance comparison-binge eating 𝛃=.38, p<.001 Athletic ideal internalization-cognitive restraint 𝛃=.21, p<.05 Athletic ideal internalization-excessive eating 𝛃=.44, p<.001 Thin ideal internalization R²=.21 Appearance comparison R²=.37 Athletic ideal internalization R²=.109 Restrictive eating R²=.119 Cognitive restraint R²=.202 Body dissatisfaction R²=.225 Excessive exercise R²=.216 Binge eating R²=.194" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2291>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "2291";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Eating attitudes and depressive symptoms were significantly associated. As for attitudes and behaviours related to physical appearance, only EAT scores suggestive of risk were significantly associated with giving up performing to avoid showing the body, feeling very affected comparing the body with others, believing that during performances the audience paid a lot of attention to their body and feeling very affected by it. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2291>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2291>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Dance_%28classical_ballet%3B_Spanish_dance%29>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Our results may be biased by the lack of sincerity among informants or by a failure to report symptoms. The groups includes students of classical ballet and Spanish dance; these two disciplines are not excatly the same: classical ballet tends to underline thinness; this fact may have introduced a bias. We cannot obtain causal conclusions. Therefore we cannot state that particular pressures and school life events trigger risk eating behaviours. The lack of objective data supporting the existence of pressure from coaches. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Further studes should analyse whether these apparent differences in type of dance (classical ballet vs Spanish dance) represent different risks for ED. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.31, p<.001";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "+";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "105";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Correlation" .

