<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2303>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "2303";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "All sport related weight pressures were significantly related to disordered eating, however only self consciousness of weight and appearance emerged as a significant correlate in the regression model. Body dissatisfaction explained an additional 5.7% of the variance in disordered eating. Sport related body dissatisfaction also emerged as a significant predictor. Positive perfectionism made a significant contribution, but negative perfectionism did not. Self esteem may not be strongly related to disordered eating. Athletic identity did not emerge as a significant variable in the final regression model, although the bivariate correlation indicated a significant positive relationship with disordered eating. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2303>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2303>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure_skating_%28singles%2C_multiple%2C_synchronized%2C_ice_dancing%2C_pairs%29>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Societal weight pressures were not exmained. Self report nature of the surveys and the willingness of athletes to honestly and accurately respond to survey items. The results are also based on a conveniance sample of skaters limited to clubs in the Midwest and Northeast. Cross sectional design, causal relationships and change over time could not be determined. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Sport psychology practiitioners should aid this effort by teaching skaters to appreciate body strengths and utilize psychological skills that may buffer the impact of negative weight pressures (e.g., utilizing positive self talk and realistic goal-setting). \nA random and more representative sample of skaters nationally and internationally, inclusive of males, should be considered in future research. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.70, p<.01";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "+";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "272";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Correlation" .

