<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_259>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "259";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Many of the psychosocial and socioemotional risk factors for disordered eating in the general population are also present in the undergraduate athlete population. Risk factors between male and Female differ in terms of presentation, but not necessarily on severity. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_259>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_259>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/N.A.>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "The present study was cross-sectional, and data were self reported from a self-selected and predominantly Caucasian sample at one undergraduate university, which limits conclusion about causality and generalizability. The EDI-3 was developed primarily with Female, which may not capture eating behavior and body shape concerns of athletes of all genders. Although many of the included instruments used in the present study have been used amply within collegiate athletes samples, only the EDI has been independently validated for this population. We did not inquire or determine eating disorder diagnosis as we were interested in disordered eating risk more generally. Finally, because we were interested in the influence of team dynamics, our classification of non-athletes included those who engaged in personal exercise but not organized exercise, which may also convey similar eating disorder risk to those involved in organized sports. Given to observed differences between athletes and non-athletes, we do not believe that engagement in personal fitness influenced the results of this study. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "The cross-sectional nature of this study precludes a conclusion about causality. Longitudinal studies are needed to clarify this relation. Given a comparatively higher focus on restrictive eating in athletes, more work is needed to confirm and better understand why Female athletes may engage in binge eating. Future reseachers should consider including the RMET as a behavioral measure of recognizing emotional expression in others that may measure different components of emotion regulation than self-report. Future researchers would do well to collect data from multiple campuses. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.315, p<.001";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "+";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "177";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Mixed sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_259>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Population>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/gender>
                "Mixed";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasStatistics>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/AgeStats_259> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/BmiStats_259> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/ExFreqStats_259> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/ExpStats_259>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSubgroup>
                "Non-athletes (N=96)\nIndividual athletes (N=45)\nTeam athletes (N=138)";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/inclusionCriteria>
                "Undergraduates at a large Mid-Atlantic university, sample of undergraduate athletes and non-athletes.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/population>
                "Athletes";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSize>
                "279";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportLevel>
                "Student";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportingPopulation>
                "Among the athletes (N=183): \nRecreational and club level (64% - N=116)\nCollegiate (35% - N=63)\nSemi professional and professional levels (1% - N=2)" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_259>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Relations>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/effectSize>
                "moderate";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasDependentVariable>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Variable_VD_259>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasIndependentVariable>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Variable_VI_259>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegreeSecondary>
                "r=.315, p<.001" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_26>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "26";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "As muscle dysmorphia increases, orthorexia nervosa increases in individuals engaged in bodybuilding sports in the TRNC. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_26>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_26>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Since this study was conducted only on male bodybuilders, the findings cannot be generalized to Female bodybuilders.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "It is essential to provide education on muscle dysmorphia, orthorexia nervosa, and the use of ergogenic nutritional supplements in Physical Education and Sports Schools, as well as in coach training programs. Additionally, training on athlete nutrition and sports psychology in these institutions would equip them with knowledge about both athlete motivations and the mental health disorders frequently seen in athletes. Given the rise of these mental health issues in gyms, sports psychologists should be present in these environments, and mental health assessments should be conducted alongside medical evaluations for new memberships.\nConducting similar studies across different sports and with female participants could contribute valuable new insights to the literature.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.28, p=.00";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "+";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "200";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Pearson correlation " .

