<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_464>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Relations> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/ComplexRelation> , <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/MediatedRelation>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/effectSize>
                "N.A.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasDependentVariable>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Variable_VD_464>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasIndependentVariable>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Variable_VI_464>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasMediator>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Variable_Mediator_464_Drive_for_muscularity%0ADietary_restraint%0ABody_satisfaction%0ANegative_affect>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegreeSecondary>
                "Sports weight pressures-general societal pressures 𝛃=.70, p<.01\nSports weight pressures-internalization 𝛃=.46, p<.01\nSports weight pressures-negative affect 𝛃=.20, p<.01\nSports weight pressure-body satisfaction 𝛃=-.18, p<.01\nSport weight pressure-dietary restraint 𝛃=.52, p<.01\nSports weight pressures-drive for muscularity 𝛃=.44, p<.01\nGeneral societal pressures-internalization 𝛃=.15, p<.01\nInternalization-body satisfaction 𝛃=-.11, p<.05\nGeneral societal pressures-negative affect 𝛃=.34, p<.01\nBody satisfaction-dietary restraint 𝛃=-.10, p<.01\nBody satisfaction-negative affect 𝛃=-.13, p<.01\nNegative affect-bulimic symptomatology 𝛃=.36, p<.01\nBody satisfaction-bulimic symptomatology 𝛃=-.08, p<.05\nDietary restraint-bulimic symptomatology 𝛃=.40, p<.01\nDrive for muscularity-bulimic symptomatology 𝛃=.12, p<.01\nInternalization R²=.34\nDrive for muscularity R²=.21\nDietary restraint R²=.32\nBody satisfaction R²=.09\nNegative affect R²=.30\nBulimic symptomatology R²=.48" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_465>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "465";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "The result of the linear regression clearly shows that the most significant explanatory weight is given by dissatisfaction with body image, despite the normal weight found. Social networks do not show a significant relationship with the risk of developing ED. We found a relationship between the risk of manifesting ED and feeling fatigued, getting little rest, and having problems concentrating. Fatigue and the risk of developing ED are not related to the hours of sports practice. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_465>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_465>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Soccer%2C_basketball%2C_handball%2C_swimming%2C_rugby%2C_volleyball>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "First, the sampling is not random and therefore does not offer representative data for each group of athletes. Second, there may be non-observational errors due to the impossibility of obtaining all the influencial variables, such as psychological variables. Third, although the survey were short, they were adiministered while medical examinations were conducted, meaning the subjects attitudes and cooperation could be rushed or hasty. Fourth, BMI is not a completely objective tool for diagnosing normal weight, underweight or overweight, particularly in the athletic population, since this value only considers weight and height, discarding other helpful information such as muscle mass and its ratio to fat mass. Fifht, we consider age to be a limiting factor since the subjects surveyed are at a stage in which numerous changes in body composition occur due to growth and social influence. In the absence of a longitudinal study, it is not possible to speak of a direct influence. Finally, sixth comprises the need for a non-athlete control group to compare the significance level of the variables in both populations. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Its necessary and interesting for prevention to be carried out before the adolescent stage (before the age of 12 or 13). \nImplement health promotion programs where aspects of body image are also addressed in addition to working on eating and sport habits. Design interventions based on age, in addition to gender, since the data are increasingly similar between male and Female in terms of body image dissatisfaction and danger of ED. In the design of interventions, pay differentiated attention to the athlete and non-athlete population. Even within the group of athletes, it is recommaleded to differentiate according to modality for diagnosis, prevention and intervention. Finally, we would like to point out, two issues: First the young people participating in the study were not diagnosed, which shows that ecessive concern about physical appearance can lead to behavior or attitudes that affect health before the disease in diagnosed. Secondly, the results suggest the need for the development of longitudinal studies in which the variability of the different dimensions analized and their degree of influence on the risk of developing ED can be observed. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "R²=.707\n𝛃=.841, p<.001";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "+";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "N.A.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Mixed sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Simple linear regression" .

