<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_545>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "545";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Dissatisfaction with body image and fat percentage influenced risk behaviour for ED in both sexes. Dissatisfaction with body image may be the primary variable affecting Females. In males, several factors influenced their EAT-26 scores. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_545>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_545>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Track_and_field%2C_basketball%2C_fencing%2C_soccer%2C_artistic_gymnastics%2C_handball%2C_judo%2C_swimming%2C_synchronised_swimming%2C_water_polo%2C_diving%2C_tae-kwon-do%2C_triathlon_and_volleyball>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "Doubly indirect method to estimate body fat, which is subject to measurement errors. The design of transversal does not permit causality inferences to be posited, meaning that there is no way to assess the intensity and direction of the associations found between the outcome and the independent variables. Participants mood states (e.g., anger, stress, anxiety and fatigue) were not controlled for in the statistical analysis of this research. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Among athletes, particularly Females, a number of unknown factors that have not been studied may explain the risk factors for eating disorders and these factors should be examined in the near future. Psychological variables, such as emotional intelligence, perfectionism, self-esteem, and depression, may better explain the variance in risk behaviour for eating disorders. Furthermore, we argue that studies should include perceptual ratings of body size in young athletes, as the deleterious health risks associated with public eating behaviours may result from distorsions in body size. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "\nBody shape questionnaire R²=.40, p<.01\ncommitment to exercise R²=.03, p<.09\nBMI R²=.02, p<.09\n% body fat R²=.03, p<.05\nBrazil economic classification criteria R²=.01, p<.24\nAge R²=.001, p<.91\nEthnicity R²=.02, p<.66\nCompetitive level R²=.01, p<.21\nAll R²=.31, p<.01";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "116 (Female)";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Mixed sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Multiple linear regression " .

