<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_632>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "632";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "In the present study, the SATAQ subdomain internalization was the only variable that could explain the variation in BOT results for male. For Female, the situation was more complex, because SPAS scores had the highest partial correlation with the BOT results, but SPAS could also explain the variation in BOT results, together with exercise frequency and both SATAQ subdomains internalization and awareness. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_632>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_632>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Fitness>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "When the study was performed, no available instruments for investigating orthorexia nervosa had been tested for validity and reliability. Because the BOT had not been validated, the authors consulted other researchers in similar areas before deciding to include the test in the present study. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Future research could use a different design, i.e., follow-up or longitudinal, to investigate fitness center participants in the areas examined in the present study, and also include a larger sample. Another important approach would be to investigate fitness center participants and orthorexia nervosa in relation to variables other than social physique anxiety and sociocultural attitudes toward appearance, in an attempt to create more knowledge of this unexplored area. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "Social physique anxiety-orthorexia nervosa: 𝛃=.20, p<.0001\nExercise frequency-orthorexia nervosa: 𝛃=.97, p<.0001\nsociocultural attitudes towards appearance questionnaire internalization-orthorexia nervosa: 𝛃=.59, p<.0001";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "Female (n=166)";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Multiple stepwise regressions" .

