<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_674>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "674";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Both population-general and athlete-specific factors have independent associations with disordered eating, including pressures (pressures for thinness and weight-loss from within sport) and psychological processes (drive for thinness, drive for leanness for performance, and athletic identity). ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_674>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_674>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/N.A.>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "The cross sectional design ; athletes were a self-selected sample, likely leading to a bias in our sample towards participants who were interested in body image, eating and exercise ; the sample primarily included Australian female athletes ; over 2000 suspected illegitimate spam responses were removed from the study.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Several findings have previously indicated small but significant effects of greater risk for athletes competing in lean versus non-lean sports, this was not replicated in the present study. This challenges notions of at-risk athletes archetypes (e.g., female athletes participating in lean sport) and narrow views of eating disorder symptomatology (e.g., low BMI, vomiting) encouraging broader screening for disodered eating accross entire athlete populations. Sporting organisations should develop and implement policies for managing athletes al all levels of risk, including primary, secondary, and tertiary interventions. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "ΔF(3,145)=11.11\nAdjusted R²=.38, p<.001\nGender 𝛃=.08, N.S.\nBMI 𝛃=.07, N.S.\nPerceived appearance based pressures from family 𝛃=.12, N.S.\nPerceived appearance based pressures from the media 𝛃=.19, N.S.\nPerceived pressure for a single ideal body in sport 𝛃=.09, N.S.\nPerceived weight pressure in sport 𝛃=.36, p<.023\nAthletes perceptions of power balances with their coach/training staff 𝛃=.03, N.S.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
