<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_843>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "843";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "There is relationships among obligatory exercise and DE. Athletic identity had a significant positive correlation with disordered eating attitudes and behaviours. Athletic identity correlated with both drive for thinness and perfectionism. The negative affectivity correlated with all the disordered eating measures. The hypothesis that athletic identity would mediate the effect of obligatory running on disordered eating was not supported. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Simple analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Correlation";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_843>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_843>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Running>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "All measures were self-report in nature. Not all the subscales of the Eating Disorder Inventory have been validated in athletic samples and there is some concern that the Eating disorder Inventory may not be accurate in identiying those athletes at risk for an eating disorder. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "One";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Obligatory and non obligatory runners were dichotomized. It is likely, however, that this form of exercise occurs on a continuum and it would be valuable to assess this. Longitudinal research of those at risk form becoming obligatory runners could determine whether th shift towards obligatory running patterns occurs immediately after adopting an exercise regimen or whether the change occurs over time as they progress in training frequency, duration, and intensity. Further research using the Athletic Identity Measurement Scale in a variety of athletic population is necessary. Future studies should examine other mediating variables related to identity (motivation to exercise are variables associated with DE and obligatory exercise). When examining obligatory runners it may be helpful to look at Deci and Ryan's (1985, 2000) self-determination theory as an explanation for obligatory exercisers motivations to engage in exercise. It would be helpful to use the regulation in exercise questionnaire in conjunction with the obligatory exercise questionnaire and eating disorder measures to evaluate in more detail why certain individuals are more susceptible to unhealthy exercise attitudes and behaviours than others. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "r=.06, p=N.S.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NS";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "179";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
