<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_890>
        a       <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/Analysis>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/analysisId>
                "890";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/authorConclusion>
                "Regression analyses indicated that increased identity with exercise contributed to decreased eating pathology amongst half marathon runners. There was a significant interaction effect noted between exercise identity and compulsive exercise, such that the relation of exercise identity and eating disorders pathology was significant amongst those runners reporting either lower or higher compulsive exercise scores. The significant associations identified for half marathoners were not demonstrated in marathon runners. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/complexityOfAnalysis>
                "Complex analyses";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/groupStatisticalAnalysis>
                "Regression";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasPopulation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_890>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasRelation>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_890>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/hasSport>
                <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Half_marathon%3B_marathon>;
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/limites>
                "It is possible that individuals registered for the half marathon were of a more elite running status. This study was also dependent on cross-sectional, self reported data collection methods. The runners assessed were all adults who were registered for competitive events in the northeastern United States, and results may not necessarily generalize to other age groups, or geographical areas, including internationally.";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/numberOfSportStudied>
                "Multiple";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/perspectives>
                "Future work should investigate the relation between the study variables over time, allowing for investigation of the impact of athlete injury. Investigate variables amongst runners who are adolescent and young adult. \nClinicians should increase screening for eating disorders and compulsive exercise across runners of less elite status, and integrate eating disorders prevention efforts amongst athletes who report low exercise identity and either lower or higher compulsive exercise. ";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDegree>
                "\n\nExercise identity 𝛃=-.21, p<.001\nCompulsive exercise 𝛃=-3.25, p<.001\nExercise identity x compulsive exercise 𝛃=.08, p<.001\nGender 𝛃=.49, p<.01\nBMI 𝛃=.08, p<.01\nR²=.38";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/relationDirection>
                "NA";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sampleSizeMobilized>
                "110";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/sportPracticeType>
                "Individual sport";
        <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/ontology/typeOfAnalysis>
                "Multiple regression" .

